WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.080
I've been working
in the fingerprint field

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.640
now for over 20 years.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:05.520
Although it can be really intricate

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.760
and time-consuming work,

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.960
it is just so fantastic when
you actually make a match.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.360
Most of us probably think that

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.000
we've got a pretty good understanding

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.040
of the basic principles

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.280
of forensic science.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.920
We binge on true crime TV series,

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.920
we read and buy huge numbers
of books,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.480
and obsessively download
true crime podcasts,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.000
trying to figure out whodunnit.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.280
But there are some things
about forensic science

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.080
that you will probably only know

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.000
if you're an insider.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.960
Let's start with some basics.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.600
Where does all the information a
forensic scientist needs come from?

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.880
Well, everywhere really.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.960
In the early 1900s,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.080
French criminologist, Edmond Locard,

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.760
declared that every contact

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.240
leaves a trace.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.400
This forms the basis

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.920
of what is now called
'Locard's exchange principle'

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.600
which says that whatever
you interact with,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.320
you leave something behind
and take something away.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:00.200
One of the areas of forensic science
where this is particularly applicable

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.600
is forensic botany.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.200
There are around 390,000 different
species of plants in the world,

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.040
each with its own pollen type,

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.360
and when it comes to crime scenes,
pollen can really tell a story.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.320
It's all around us,
it's on the ground,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.800
if you walk on soil or vegetation

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.200
you pick it up,
inevitably you pick it up.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.120
Patricia Wiltshire
is a forensic ecologist,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.360
she uses pollen to help solve crimes.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.600
Unlike other forms of evidence,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.800
pollen isn't easily washed away.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.840
It gets into clothes and it can often
be found on shoes

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.160
or on the foot pedals in cars.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.000
Pollen and spores are too small
to be seen by the naked eye,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.680
so criminals rarely realise

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.440
that they've picked them up
at a crime scene.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.360
Pollen and spores are produced
by plants and fungi.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.280
They grow in specific places,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.080
so you know very well that this plant
will grow in this soil,

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.760
that plant will grow in that soil,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:55.640
and because of that, we can predict
where they are from.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.960
In one particular case
of an attempted murder,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.960
this man tried to strangle a girl
underneath a lamp post

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.240
and he said he hadn't been there,
of course,

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.280
but by taking his clothing apart,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.960
I showed that he'd bumped up against
a fence with his left shoulder,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.280
that he'd dragged her
through a hedge,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.000
that he'd knelt, and so on.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.880
And because I sampled
the crime scene in detail,

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.240
I could see where
bits of his body had been

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.640
so I could actually reconstruct
what he did at the time.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.120
There are other ways in which nature
can help forensic scientists

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.280
determine when a crime was committed.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.400
Insects, for example, can provide
a lot of information

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:33.040
if you know what to look for,

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.800
as forensic entomologist,
Amoret Whitaker, explains.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.480
When you die your body starts
to break down and decompose

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.440
basically straight away. And so your
body starts giving off certain odours

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.480
and those are very attractive
to blowflies.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:52.200
If we can work out how old the larvae
are that are feeding on the body,

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:56.360
then we can work out the minimum time
that that person must have been dead.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:58.920
The colder it is, the slower the
larvae develop,

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.240
the warmer it is,
the faster they develop.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:03.880
Really, the shorter the time span
since the death,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.320
the more accurate we can be.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:09.040
Remember Edmond Locard, the French
criminologist we mentioned earlier?

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.120
His contribution to forensic science

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.080
didn't end at
"everything leaves a trace".

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.080
He developed and contributed to
various methods of forensic analysis,

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.400
including dactylography,
the study of fingerprints.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.960
The interpretation of evidence
has changed a lot

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.040
since I became a crime scene examiner
20 years ago.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.120
Back then, if I examined a car whose
door had been broken open

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.040
to gain entry, and the
only evidence I found

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.960
was a fingermark on the
outside of the driver's door,

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:37.560
I could reasonably expect

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.280
that if the fingermark was
identified for someone,

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.560
then that person would eventually
be charged with the offence

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.440
and the case would go to court.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.360
However, things are
now very different.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:50.720
If the same fingermark
was found today,

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.000
especially in a relatively
non-serious case such as this,

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.440
there would be a strong likelihood
that it wouldn't go to court

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.440
and this is because the
Crown Prosecution Service, or CPS,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.960
they need to be satisfied that
there is sufficient evidence

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.320
to provide a realistic prospect
of conviction.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.240
Their argument for not prosecuting

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.360
based on the fingermark
on the outside of the vehicle

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.640
might be that anyone could have
walked by and leaned on the vehicle,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.080
leaving the mark.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.400
In the past,

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:24.680
defence barristers argued about
who the evidence belonged to,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:27.360
but now they focus on how
the evidence got there.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:28.680
Could the evidence have been left

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.480
by someone other than
the person breaking into the car?

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.680
If there is any possibility
it could be someone else,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:38.160
then the CPS tend not
to take these cases to court.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.720
Forensic science
is constantly evolving.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.840
In the future, it's possible that
many crimes will be solved

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.760
before they've even been committed,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.000
as predictive software will mean
that police forces

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:52.240
will be able to anticipate when and
where a crime is likely to take place

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.400
and even who's likely to commit it.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:57.840
Then there's our microbiome,
the tiny microbes in our gut,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:59.880
which, according to
a new study Harvard,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.880
can identify us as individuals
just like a fingerprint.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.520
Despite the rapidly changing
world of forensics,

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.960
Linda says that there's one thing

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.200
that good forensic scientists
never forget...

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.440
That we're dealing
with real people's lives,

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:13.720
and we need to ensure
that we work

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.320
in an objective and unbiased way,

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.440
because if we get it wrong, it's
people's liberty that's at stake.

